# A Network’s Hidden Arithmetic

Prime cycles, eigenvalues, and quadratic zeros form three views of one local phenomenon. Begin with the visual experiment below: move $\lambda$ while holding $q>0$ and watch the roots cross from a conjugate pair on the critical circle into unequal real zeros.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

[Learn more about the Ihara zeta function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ihara_zeta_function) and [spectral graph theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spectral_graph_theory).

## 1. Generate the spectral coefficients

The reciprocal roots satisfy $\alpha+\beta=\lambda$ and $\alpha\beta=q$. Their power sums $S_n=\alpha^n+\beta^n$ can be generated without radicals.

{{algorithm:0}}

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the recurrence proof</summary>Both reciprocal roots solve $x^2-\lambda x+q=0$. Multiply by $x^n$ and add the identities for $x=\alpha$ and $x=\beta$ to obtain $S_{n+2}=\lambda S_{n+1}-qS_n$.</details>

## 2. Inspect the critical circle

When $\lambda^2\le 4q$, the reciprocal roots are conjugates with product $q$, so each has modulus $\sqrt q$. Their reciprocals—the local zeros—therefore lie on $|u|=1/\sqrt q$.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the angular parametrization</summary>Write $\lambda=2\sqrt q\cos\theta$. Then $\alpha=\sqrt q e^{i\theta}$ and $\beta=\sqrt q e^{-i\theta}$, giving $S_n=2q^{n/2}\cos(n\theta)$ and $|S_n|\le2q^{n/2}$.</details>

{{visualization:1}}

## 3. Watch the explicit formula cancel

Multiplication by $1-\lambda u+qu^2$ kills every interior coefficient of the truncated series. Only $\lambda-2qu$ and two upper-boundary terms remain.

{{algorithm:2}}

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the exact identity and its scope</summary>For $T_N(u)=\sum_{k=0}^N S_{k+1}u^k$, one has $$(1-\lambda u+qu^2)T_N(u)=\lambda-2qu-S_{N+2}u^{N+1}+qS_{N+1}u^{N+2}.$$ This is a theorem about one local spectral factor. Global primitive-cycle counts additionally require a non-backtracking trace theorem, Möbius inversion, and the Bass determinant identity.</details>

## 4. Where to go next

The natural global comparison is between primitive cycles of length $n$ and the scale $q^n/n$. Any comparison with integer primes must state a normalization. Explore [Ramanujan graphs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramanujan_graph) for the spectral setting and [Lucas sequences](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucas_sequence) for the recurrence setting.

